SC GOVERNMENT SECURITY WATCH — October 23, 2026

One active incident found affecting South Carolina municipal government. No new incidents affecting SC state agencies or other counties/municipalities today.

INCIDENT: City of Columbia, SC — Cyberattack / Network Intrusion

Entity Affected: City of Columbia, South Carolina (state capital, pop. ~150,000) — municipal government.

What Happened: Columbia's IT department flagged suspicious activity on the city network and brought in SLED (SC Law Enforcement Division) to investigate. Several city applications and online services were taken down, including the MyColaSC resident portal (utility bill payment, non-emergency reporting), Business Licensing, Parks & Recreation, Planning & Development Services, Geographic Information Systems (GIS), and Animal Services. The city says critical public safety and utility systems — police, Columbia-Richland Fire, 911 communications, Columbia Water, and the Water payment center — remain operational and were not affected. No data theft/exposure has been confirmed publicly. No threat actor or ransomware group had claimed responsibility as of the most recent reporting, and the city has not characterized the attack as ransomware. Investigation is ongoing with no restoration timeline given; residents were told to call (803) 545-3300 for issues normally filed through MyColaSC.

When/Status: First reported Oct 20-21, 2026; follow-up coverage Oct 22, 2026. As of this run (Oct 23), still actively under investigation — no public update on attribution, root cause, or full restoration.

Detection/Hunting Angle: Nature of the intrusion (vector, whether ransomware/data exfil) is still unconfirmed, so I'm not assigning specific ATT&CK technique IDs here rather than guess. Worth watching for a follow-up claim from a ransomware group (would immediately imply T1486/Impact) and for any confirmation of initial access vector (public-facing web portal like MyColaSC is a plausible entry point — worth a sanity check against your own external-facing web app logging/WAF coverage for similar patterns: anomalous auth attempts, unexpected admin logins, or mass data pulls on resident-portal applications).

